                    "Using your UPMC Health Plan Login to change your preferences is quick and easy, and you can make the change through the UPMC Health Plan app or member site:",
                    "When you choose to get your plan materials online, you have safe and easy access to your documents 24/7. Instead of waiting for us to mail you documents like your Explanation of Benefits (EOBs), we will send you an email or text message with a link to see your documents via your computer, smartphone, or tablet. (You'll need to log in to the UPMC Health Plan member site to see your documents.)",
                   
                    "Once you register for an account and log in, you can:\r\nCommunicate with your UPMC providers.\r\nSchedule and manage your in-person and virtual appointments.\r\nView your medical record, providers' notes, and test results.\r\nRenew your prescriptions.\r\nPay bills you've received from UPMC providers.\r\nGet select information about your health plan.",
                    "Visit MyUPMC.com to access your account or download the app from the or",
                    "Schedule and manage your in-person and virtual appointments.",
                    "Pay bills you've received from UPMC providers.",
                    "Get select information about your health plan.",
                   
                   
                    "Live chat or call our award-winning Health Care Concierge team to:\r\nGet help finding a provider in your network.\r\nDetermine whether a prescription drug is covered (if applicable).\r\nLearn about preventive services you may need.\r\nAnd more!",
                    "You can chat with a Health Care Concierge through the UPMC Health Plan app. If you want to speak with one over the phone, call the number on your member ID card. Check your card for the team's hours of operation. TTY users should call 711.",
                    "Contact the UPMC Health Plan Tech Guide team at 1-833-685-5418 (TTY: 711) and schedule an appointment Monday through Friday from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. These experts can help you use all the free digital health care tools we offer. They can even help you with basic troubleshooting and commonly used features on your personal devices!",
                    "Get help finding a provider in your network.",
                    "Determine whether a prescription drug is covered (if applicable).",
                    "Learn about preventive services you may need.",
                    "And more!",
                   
                    "Before you seek care from providers, we recommend that you review your plan documents. You can find them by going to the Coverage Overview on UPMC Health Plan member site or logging in to the UPMC Health Plan app.\r\n\r\nAfter you select your plan, you will see a grid that outlines some of your most commonly used benefits, copayments for different types of providers, your coinsurance, and your deductible (if applicable).\r\n\r\nFor detailed information about your plan, see your Schedule of Benefits for your plan. This document:\r\nExplains your copayment, deductible, and coinsurance amounts, as well as your out-of-pocket maximum.\r\nLists health care services and their level of coverage.",
                    "Preventive care and screenings can help you stay healthy, reduce your risk of developing diseases, and keep conditions you have from becoming worse. All this may reduce your future health care expenses. Contact our Health Care Concierge team for help verifying your coverage.\r\nWant to know if you are due for a screening?\r\nCheck out your recommended care",
                    "UPMC Health Plan covers many preventive care visits, screenings, and vaccines at no cost for members of all ages. This means you won’t have to pay anything. However, if your visit becomes diagnostic in nature—that is, if your no-cost physical turns into an appointment to treat bronchitis—you could be billed for that portion of the visit.\r\nCheck our list of preventive services and guidelines.",
                    "Cost sharing is where you and UPMC Health Plan will share the cost of your care. This means you must pay a portion of your health care expenses and UPMC Health Plan will pay a portion.\r\nYou have to pay your deductible before UPMC Health Plan will start covering your care costs. This means you'll pay for each covered service until you reach your deductible amount.\r\nCopayments are flat fees you pay at the time of service. If your plan requires you to pay a copayment, you must pay this flat fee at the time you receive a service. Your deductible and coinsurance typically do not apply to services with a copayment.\r\nCoinsurance is the amount you pay for covered services after you meet your deductible. Your medical plan coverage will pay the rest. It is expressed as a percentage. If your coinsurance is 20 percent, you must pay 20 percent of the cost of covered health care services. Your medical plan coverage will pay the remaining 80 percent.\r\nYou will continue to pay your share of the cost of covered services you receive from in-network providers (or coinsurance) until you meet your out-of-pocket maximum. This is the most you will pay for covered health care expenses in a plan year. If you meet your out-of-pocket maximum, UPMC Health Plan will pay for 100 percent of your covered care for the remainder of the plan year.\r\nYou can view all of your plan's care costs by going to Coverage Overview on the UPMC Health Plan member site.",
                    "The provider will send UPMC Health Plan a claim.\r\nWe will process the claim based on your plan.\r\nWe will send you a notification to review your Explanation of Benefits (EOB) online or mail you a paper copy. Your EOB will summarize the cost of your care, detail what UPMC Health Plan paid, and list what you may owe your provider. EOBs are not bills, and you do not need to pay anything when you receive one.\r\nAfter the provider receives a payment from us, they will know how much to bill you (if anything).",
                    "Switch to electronic EOBs to save time and trees!\r\nUpdate your preferences to be notified about new EOBs by email or text message. You can choose this option by using the UPMC Health Plan app or member site.",
                    "Our EOB tutorial can help you understand this important document.",
                    "If you receive care from a participating provider, you will not have to submit a claim to UPMC Health Plan. UPMC Health Plan will pay the provider directly. However, if you obtain medically necessary covered services from a nonparticipating provider, you may have to file a claim yourself.\r\n\r\nYou must notify UPMC Health Plan in writing within 20 days of the occurrence or the beginning of any loss covered by the policy, or as soon thereafter as is reasonably possible. Notice given by you or on your behalf should be sent to Claims Department, UPMC Health Plan, PO Box 2999, Pittsburgh, PA 15230-2999. You may also call the Health Plan at the number on your member ID card, or contact any authorized agent of UPMC Health Plan and provide them with enough information to identify you.\r\n\r\nTo submit a claim, go to Documents and Forms on the UPMC Health Plan member site.",
                    "UPMC Health Plan offers customized help with lifestyle and condition management, as well as shared decision support services. These health and wellness services are personalized to help you meet your goals. And they're free for all UPMC Health Plan members!\r\nOur health coaches can work with you to achieve your health-related goals. They will help you stay motivated and keep you accountable. Members of the team hold professional degrees and licenses as registered nurses, counselors, diabetes educators, dietitians, social workers, exercise physiologists, and health education specialists.",
                    "Condition management topics include but are not limited to:\r\nDiabetes.\r\nCardiovascular health.\r\nRespiratory health.\r\nBehavioral health.\r\nChronic pain.",
                    "With lifestyle management health coaching, you can work with our experts one-on-one or in a group to set goals for healthy living. Coaches will help you lower your risk for chronic conditions and manage conditions you already have. Health coaches will also work with you to personalize your experience and focus on goals and strategies that work best for you.\r\nProgram areas include:\r\nWeight management.\r\nPhysical Activity.\r\nTobacco cessation.\r\nStress management.\r\nNutrition basics.\r\nDiabetes prevention.\r\nHealthy families.\r\nSleep.\r\nLifestyle management is available to all members. You can get help by phone, or virtually via the Health Coaching and Clinical Navigation bookings page.\r\nCondition management involves receiving education and support from our expert care managers. They provide tools to help you understand your health, manage a new or chronic condition, and learn ways to partner with your providers.\r\nCondition management topics include but are not limited to:\r\nDiabetes.\r\nCardiovascular health.\r\nRespiratory health.\r\nBehavioral health.\r\nChronic pain.\r\nPodcast: What is a care manager and how can they help me?\r\nMandi Batykefer, care manager for the Commercial-dedicated team at UPMC Health Plan, tells us who the care managers are at UPMC Health Plan and what they do to support members.\r\n\"Health Break:\" What is a care manager and how can they help me? | UPMC MyHealth Matters\r\nAdditional care management programs include:\r\nThe Baby Steps maternity health coaching program.\r\nPediatric care management.\r\nCase management.\r\nCondition management is available to all members. You can get help by phone, or virtually via the Health Coaching and Clinical Navigation bookings page.\r\nShared decision making allows you and your health care provider to discuss your treatment options.\r\nUnderstanding your goals of care while taking into consideration your values is important when it comes to:\r\nOptional surgeries such as hip, knee, or bariatric surgery.\r\nCondition or pain management.\r\nCancer treatment.",
